(memory "ui/reactive" :when "a view needs a dropdown, toggle or filter that updates it at once, without asking the agent again")
Reactive controls: :on-change on ui/select or ui/checkbox. One-argument
function, run when user picks or ticks. No submit, no model turn. Gets same
alist as form handler, answers by rendering:

(defun panel (window)
  (ui/stack
    (ui/select '("7" "30" "90") :name "d" :value window
      :on-change (lambda (values) (ui/render (panel (cdr (assoc "d" values))))))
    (ui/table (issues-since window))))
(ui/render (panel "7"))

Text fields have no :on-change on purpose: each keystroke would round-trip,
redrawing the field being typed in. Put ui/input in ui/form, let submit
carry it.

Button INSIDE a form gets the same alist, if its action takes one argument.

(memory "ui/actions" :when "a button in a view must keep working after a redraw, or an old view's controls must be dropped, or a click just reached a stale view")
Actions close over their environment. REPL persistent, so
(setq counter (+ counter 1)) in a handler sticks; a later step reads it like
any global. They die with the REPL: clearing conversation drops every action.

Action may be a quoted call to a global function instead: '(refresh-dashboard)
or '(set-filter "open"). Args passed as written, not evaluated. Field alist
appended when the function takes one more argument. Function looked up by name
on click, so redefine it to change what click does. Never dropped past 500
handlers, kept across REPL restart. Prefer it for long-lived views:

(defun set-filter (f) (ui/render (panel f)))
(ui/button "open only" '(set-filter "open"))

(ui/forget view) drops actions behind view's controls; view shows expired,
clicks run nothing. Use on a view you replaced or closed. Returns count
dropped.

(memory "ui/send" :when "a view needs a control that hands a request back to the agent along with what the user entered")
(ui/send text...) arguments join as echo joins them. The result joins
conversation as user message, so you take the next turn and answer normally:

(ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/send "search the issues for" (cdr (assoc "q" values))))
  (ui/input :name "q" :label "search")
  :submit "ask")

Send for judgement, handle in Lisp for work. Prose request, or a decision or
step with no code written, needs a turn. Filtering, paging, recomputing, calling
a loaded tool is free. Handler may render AND send: render what user sees while
you think, then send. Several sends in one handler join into ONE message: one
click, one thing. Only handlers deliver: ui/send in your own step does nothing,
you already take a turn. Write a request, "search the issues for auth", not a
note, "user clicked search".

(memory "ui/panels" :when "a file or view must open beside the chat, or a tab must be shown, moved, focused or closed")
Panel beside chat: tabs in splits, up to 3 across, 3 down. Folder is
"attachments" or "knowledge". :split is "here" (focused split, default),
"right" or "down" (new split beside focused one).
- (ui/open-file folder name [:split "right"]) opens file preview in tab.
  List folder first: unknown name opens tab saying so. Open file gets focus,
  no second tab. Returns tab id.
- (ui/open-view title view [:split "down"]) widget in tab, not under message.
  Controls run in this REPL as usual. Same title replaces that tab. Returns
  tab id.
- (ui/close tab) · (ui/focus tab) by id open returned. ui/close gives t if tab
  was open, nil if not.
- (ui/tabs) lists panel tabs: (:id id :kind :file|:view|:chat|:page :title
  title :shown t :focused t). User opens, closes, moves tabs too: read it
  before closing or focusing by id from earlier step.
- (ui/file-link text folder name) widget; click opens file in tab. List
  files user can open:

  (ui/render (ui/stack (ui/file-link "Q3 report" "knowledge" "q3.pdf")
                       (ui/file-link "photo" "attachments" "cat.png")))

  Open for user when they ask to see file; link when offering several.

(memory "ui/build" :when "the agent is about to build a visual view, dashboard, chart or interactive page")
Build ui view and test it without rendering. ui/render only once it works.

(memory "ui/basics" :when "the agent is about to show a table, form or buttons in chat, or any view the user can click or type into")
Example panel, a form whose action takes the alist, and a button whose action takes none:

(defun panel ()
  (ui/stack
    (ui/heading "Open issues")
    (ui/table (head issues-1 10))
    (ui/form (lambda (values) (ui/render (results (cdr (assoc "q" values)))))
      (ui/input :name "q" :label "filter" :placeholder "auth")
      :submit "search")))
(ui/render (panel))

**Actions** are ordinary Lisp functions. Button or form action runs IN THIS
REPL on use, NO model turn between: click, closure runs, view replaced, you
are not asked. So every branch you anticipate is free.
- Button action takes no argument: (lambda () (ui/render (panel))). Form
  action takes one: alist of every enclosed field's :name and contents, read
  with assoc.
- Action answers via ui/render, replacing view in place; rendering nothing
  leaves view as is.
- Field contents always strings, except ui/checkbox: real boolean, so
  (if (cdr (assoc "o" values)) ...) works. (read s) turns string into number.
  "" and "false" are both TRUE, hence boolean tick box.
- Filtering, paging, recomputing, calling a loaded tool is free. Prose request,
  or a decision with no code written, needs a turn.
- Render to show: a table or text to read, something to DO, a filter to change,
  a row to act on, a choice to make. Long data: write a file and open it beside
  chat. Recall memory ui/panels for how.
- End with a short final answer in prose that points at what you rendered, as
  in "the 27 open issues are in the table above", without repeating it.
- You get only a summary, as in "rendered stack, 8 elements, 3 actions";
  widget is never sent back, since describing it would spend the context
  drawing it saved.
